# Batch 26 (2026-11-13): volcanoes, earthquakes, tsunamis and epidemics; forgotten Bronze Age and island civilisations
# (Shimao, Hohokam, Marajoara, Nuragic Sardinia, Talayotic Menorca, Elam, Magan, Akrotiri, Ubaid);
# UFO earth-lights, official UFO studies and famous photos; literary forgeries and fake 'Old World' inscriptions in America
H,M='history','myth'
def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  return d
E=[]
# --- part 1: volcanoes, earthquakes, tsunamis and epidemics
E+=[
X('laki-1783','Laki 1783: the Icelandic fissure eruption that poisoned a continent',H,'confirmed',2,1783,['textual','geological/ice core'],
 "On 8 June 1783 a row of more than 130 craters, the Lakagígar fissure in southern Iceland, began an eruption that lasted until February 1784. It poured out roughly 14–15 cubic kilometres of basalt lava and released an estimated 120 million tonnes of sulphur dioxide. Fluorine poisoning and the loss of grazing killed around half to three-quarters of Iceland's cattle, horses and sheep, and the famine that followed, the Móðuharðindin ('Mist Hardships'), killed roughly a fifth of Iceland's people. A sulphurous 'dry fog' spread over Europe in summer 1783; parish registers in England and France show a spike in deaths, and Benjamin Franklin, then in Paris, speculated that the haze had cooled the following winter, an early suggestion that volcanoes can change climate.",
 [('The Fire Sermon','On 20 July 1783 the pastor Jón Steingrímsson preached his famous eldmessa (\'fire sermon\') at Kirkjubæjarklaustur as the lava approached; the flow stopped short of the church, which local tradition remembered as an answer to prayer. Geologists see it as a flow that stalled where it spread out on flatter ground.'),
  ('A cause of the French Revolution?','Some writers have linked the Laki years to the poor harvests and unrest that preceded 1789. Historians note that the key harvest failure came later (1788, worsened by a hailstorm), so any link is indirect and debated.')],
 ['wiki:Laki','doi:10.1029/2001JD002042'],img='Laki'),
X('event-8200','The 8.2-kiloyear event: when a draining ice-age lake chilled the North Atlantic',H,'confirmed',2,-6200,['geological/ice core','radiocarbon'],
 "Greenland ice cores record a sharp cold spell about 8,200 years ago (c. 6200 BCE) that lasted roughly 150 years, the largest abrupt cooling of the Holocene. The leading explanation is that the ice dam holding back the vast glacial Lake Agassiz–Ojibway in North America failed, sending a pulse of fresh water through Hudson Bay into the North Atlantic, which weakened the ocean circulation that carries heat northward. The same period saw a rapid rise in sea level, and Neolithic communities from the Near East to Europe show signs of change around this time, although how far the climate drove those changes is debated.",
 [('Flood myths','Some writers suggest that sudden sea-level rise around 8,200 years ago, along with the near-contemporary Storegga tsunami in the North Sea, could lie behind flood stories. This is speculative: the event left no written record, and flood myths have many possible sources.'),
  ('Driver of migration','Some archaeologists argue the cold, dry spell pushed early farmers out of Anatolia and the Levant towards Europe; others find little sign of disruption at many sites and see the timing as coincidence.')],
 ['wiki:8.2-kiloyear_event','wiki:Lake_Agassiz'],img='Lake Agassiz',dateNote='c. 8,200 years before present'),
X('armero-1985','Armero 1985: the town buried by a volcano everyone had been warned about',H,'confirmed',2,1985,['textual'],
 "On the night of 13 November 1985 a fairly small eruption of Nevado del Ruiz in Colombia melted part of the summit ice cap. Mudflows of water, ice and volcanic debris (lahars) rushed down the river valleys and, about two and a half hours later, buried the town of Armero, killing around 23,000 of its roughly 29,000 inhabitants. Scientists had published a hazard map weeks earlier showing Armero in the danger zone, and the town stood on deposits from earlier lahars in 1595 and 1845, but evacuation was never ordered. The slow death of 13-year-old Omayra Sánchez, trapped in the debris and photographed by Frank Fournier, became one of the most famous images of the decade. The disaster led directly to the creation of the USGS–USAID Volcano Disaster Assistance Program.",
 [('Forgotten warnings','Armero is often cited as a case where history repeated: the 1845 lahar had also destroyed settlements on the same site. Researchers use it to show how quickly the memory of rare disasters fades.'),
  ('A place of pilgrimage','The ruins are now a memorial; Omayra Sánchez\'s grave site has become an informal shrine where visitors leave offerings and ask for favours, a form of folk devotion that the Catholic Church has not formally recognised.')],
 ['wiki:Armero_tragedy','wiki:Nevado_del_Ruiz'],img='Armero tragedy'),
X('messina-1908','Messina 1908: Europe\'s deadliest earthquake, in the strait of Scylla and Charybdis',H,'confirmed',2,1908,['textual','geological/ice core'],
 "Early on 28 December 1908 an earthquake of about magnitude 7.1 struck the Strait of Messina between Sicily and Calabria. Within minutes a tsunami with waves of up to about 12 metres hit both shores. The cities of Messina and Reggio Calabria were almost completely destroyed, and estimates of the death toll range from about 75,000 to over 100,000, making it one of the deadliest earthquakes in recorded European history. Relief came from across the world, including Russian and British sailors who were among the first to help. The strait is the same narrow channel where Greek myth placed the monster Scylla and the whirlpool Charybdis, and where the Fata Morgana mirage is famously seen.",
 [('Myth and real danger','Some writers note that ancient stories of a deadly strait may reflect its real hazards: strong tidal currents and whirlpools, and an earthquake-prone coast. The 1908 quake shows the hazard is real, but there is no evidence linking the myth to any particular earthquake.')],
 ['wiki:1908_Messina_earthquake'],img='1908 Messina earthquake'),
X('kanto-1923','The Great Kantō earthquake (1923): fire, rumour and massacre',H,'confirmed',2,1923,['textual'],
 "At 11:58 on 1 September 1923 a magnitude-7.9 earthquake struck the Kantō plain, devastating Tokyo and Yokohama just as charcoal stoves were lit for lunch. Fires driven by typhoon winds caused most of the deaths; at the former Army Clothing Depot in Tokyo, where tens of thousands had taken refuge, a fire whirl killed about 38,000 people. Around 105,000 people died in total. In the chaos, false rumours spread that Koreans were poisoning wells and setting fires, and vigilante groups, sometimes with police or soldiers, murdered thousands of Koreans as well as Chinese residents and some Japanese mistaken for them; the exact number is still disputed. 1 September is now Japan's Disaster Prevention Day.",
 [('Rumour and scapegoats','Historians see the massacre as a classic case of disaster rumour turning into violence against an outsider group, comparable to accusations of well-poisoning against Jews during the Black Death. Japanese governments have been criticised for not acknowledging it fully.'),
  ('Divine warning','Some contemporaries, including the businessman Shibusawa Eiichi, called the quake tenken, a punishment from heaven for the luxury of modern Tokyo, echoing older Japanese ideas about earthquakes as moral warnings.')],
 ['wiki:1923_Great_Kantō_earthquake','wiki:Kantō_Massacre'],img='1923 Great Kantō earthquake'),
X('tangshan-1976','Tangshan 1976: the earthquake that came in a year of omens',H,'confirmed',2,1976,['textual'],
 "Before dawn on 28 July 1976 an earthquake of about magnitude 7.6 destroyed the industrial city of Tangshan in northern China while most people slept. The official death toll is 242,769, and some outside estimates are higher, making it one of the deadliest earthquakes of the 20th century. A year earlier, in February 1975, officials had evacuated Haicheng hours before a major quake after foreshocks and reports of odd animal behaviour, a success widely publicised as earthquake prediction; at Tangshan there was no such warning. China refused foreign aid at the time. 1976 also saw the deaths of Zhou Enlai, Zhu De and, in September, Mao Zedong.",
 [('Mandate of Heaven','In traditional Chinese thought, great earthquakes could be read as signs that a ruler was losing heaven\'s favour. Many people at the time connected Tangshan with Mao\'s death weeks later; historians treat this as popular interpretation, not a causal link.'),
  ('Predicting quakes from animals','Reports of restless animals, changing well water and strange lights before Haicheng and Tangshan fuelled interest in natural precursors. Seismologists consider Haicheng a special case helped by a strong foreshock sequence; reliable short-term prediction has not been achieved.')],
 ['wiki:1976_Tangshan_earthquake','wiki:1975_Haicheng_earthquake'],img='1976 Tangshan earthquake'),
X('smong-2004','Smong and the laboon: how oral tradition saved lives in the 2004 tsunami',H,'confirmed',1,2004,['oral tradition','textual'],
 "On 26 December 2004 a magnitude-9.1 earthquake off Sumatra sent tsunamis across the Indian Ocean, killing about 230,000 people in 14 countries. On Simeulue island, very close to the epicentre, only seven people died out of roughly 78,000, largely because islanders remembered the word smong, from stories and songs about a tsunami in 1907: when the ground shakes and the sea draws back, run to the hills. In Thailand and Myanmar, Moken sea nomads also moved to high ground, drawing on traditions of the laboon, a destructive wave sent by the spirits of ancestors. Scientists now treat such traditions as valuable sources for disaster planning.",
 [('Deep memory in stories','Researchers point to Simeulue alongside Japanese tsunami stones and Pacific Northwest oral histories of the 1700 Cascadia quake as evidence that oral tradition can preserve practical warnings for generations.'),
  ('Myth as explanation','For the Moken the laboon is also part of their cosmology, sent to cleanse the world; outsiders often reduce it to a warning, while the Moken see it within a wider spiritual understanding.')],
 ['wiki:2004_Indian_Ocean_earthquake_and_tsunami','doi:10.1193/1.2204966','wiki:Moken'],img='2004 Indian Ocean earthquake and tsunami'),
X('samalas-1257','Samalas 1257: the mystery eruption traced to a lost kingdom on Lombok',H,'confirmed',2,1257,['geological/ice core','textual','radiocarbon'],
 "Ice cores from both Greenland and Antarctica contain a huge sulphate spike dating to about 1257–1258, the largest of the past 2,000 years, but for decades nobody knew which volcano had caused it. In 2013 a team led by Franck Lavigne identified Samalas, part of the Rinjani volcanic complex on the Indonesian island of Lombok, using radiocarbon dates, the spread of its ash and pumice, and the Babad Lombok, Old Javanese texts written on palm leaves that describe the destruction of Pamatan, the capital of a Lombok kingdom. The eruption left the caldera that now holds the crater lake Segara Anak. European chronicles record a cold, wet year and famine in 1258.",
 [('An Indonesian Pompeii?','Pamatan has been described in the press as a possible buried \'Pompeii\' of the East. Its remains have not been located or excavated, so this remains a hope rather than a finding.'),
  ('London\'s mass graves','Mass burials at Spitalfields in London were once linked to the 1258 famine and the eruption; later radiocarbon work suggested some of the burials may be earlier, so the connection is uncertain.')],
 ['wiki:Mount_Samalas','doi:10.1073/pnas.1307520110'],img='Mount Rinjani',changed="2013: the 'mystery eruption' of 1257, known only from ice cores since the 1980s–90s, was pinned to Samalas."),
X('huaynaputina-1600','Huaynaputina 1600: the Andean eruption felt as famine in Russia',H,'confirmed',3,1600,['textual','geological/ice core'],
 "On 19 February 1600 Huaynaputina, an inconspicuous volcano in southern Peru, exploded in the largest eruption in recorded South American history. Ash and pyroclastic flows buried several villages, killed around 1,500 people locally and devastated the vineyards and farms of the Moquegua valley; Spanish and Indigenous accounts describe days of darkness in Arequipa. Its sulphate aerosol cooled the Northern Hemisphere: 1601 was one of the coldest summers in six centuries, and the eruption is linked with crop failures in Europe and especially with the Russian famine of 1601–1603, which killed perhaps a third of Russia's population and helped bring on the Time of Troubles.",
 [('Appeasing the mountain','Contemporary chronicles report that local people made offerings and sacrifices to the volcano before and during the eruption, treating it as a powerful being, a belief documented for many Andean mountains.'),
  ('How much was the volcano to blame?','Historians agree the cold summer harmed harvests, but the scale of the Russian disaster also reflected politics, hoarding and the weakness of Boris Godunov\'s rule.')],
 ['wiki:Huaynaputina','wiki:Russian_famine_of_1601–1603'],img='Huaynaputina'),
X('cocoliztli-1545','Cocoliztli: the great pestilence of New Spain and a 2018 DNA clue',H,'confirmed',2,1545,['textual','genetic'],
 "In 1545–1548 and again in 1576–1578 epidemics that the Nahua called cocoliztli ('pestilence') swept through Mexico, killing millions; the first outbreak may have killed as much as 45 per cent of the native population. Spanish doctors such as Francisco Hernández described high fevers, bleeding and jaundice, and the disease killed far more Indigenous people than Spaniards. Its cause has long been debated. In 2018 a study of DNA from victims buried at Teposcolula-Yucundaa in Oaxaca found Salmonella enterica Paratyphi C, a cause of enteric fever, suggesting it was one of the pathogens involved.",
 [('A native haemorrhagic fever?','The epidemiologist Rodolfo Acuña-Soto and colleagues argued that cocoliztli was an Indigenous viral haemorrhagic fever spread by rodents and triggered by severe drought, citing tree-ring evidence. This remains a competing explanation, since the DNA method cannot detect every possible pathogen.'),
  ('Divine judgement','Contemporaries interpreted the epidemics through religion: friars saw them as God\'s will, while Indigenous accounts mourned them as the ending of a world.')],
 ['wiki:Cocoliztli_epidemics','doi:10.1038/s41559-017-0446-6'],img='Cocoliztli epidemics',end=1578),
]
# --- part 2: forgotten Bronze Age and island civilisations
E+=[
X('shimao','Shimao: the stone city mistaken for the Great Wall',H,'confirmed',2,-2300,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "Shimao, in Shenmu County on the northern Loess Plateau of Shaanxi, China, is the largest known walled settlement of its time in China, covering about 400 hectares and occupied roughly 2300–1800 BCE, at the end of the Longshan period. Its stone walls were long thought to be part of the Great Wall, until looted jades led archaeologists to investigate in the 2010s. At its centre stands the Huangchengtai, a terraced stone platform about 70 metres high, decorated with carved stone faces and creatures; jade objects were set into the walls, and pits near the East Gate held the skulls of dozens of young people, many of them women, probably sacrificed. Shimao challenges the older view that Chinese civilisation grew only from the Central Plains.",
 [('The Xia question','Some Chinese scholars have linked Shimao with traditions of early rulers, including the Yellow Emperor or the Xia dynasty; others caution that no writing has been found to connect the site with any named people.'),
  ('A lost pyramid city','Online, Shimao\'s stepped platform is sometimes presented as a \'Chinese pyramid\' built by a forgotten advanced culture. Archaeologists see it as the work of a powerful local Neolithic polity linked to steppe and Yellow River exchange networks.')],
 ['wiki:Shimao','wiki:Longshan_culture'],img='Shimao',end=-1800,changed="2012 onward: excavations showed the 'Great Wall' ruins were a Neolithic city; the faces on the Huangchengtai were published from 2018."),
X('hohokam','The Hohokam: canal builders of the Sonoran Desert',H,'confirmed',2,450,['archaeological','oral tradition'],
 "The Hohokam culture flourished in the Sonoran Desert of what is now southern Arizona from roughly the 5th century to about 1450 CE. In the Salt and Gila river valleys its people dug hundreds of kilometres of irrigation canals, the largest pre-Columbian canal system in North America, some stretches of which modern Phoenix canals still follow. They built ballcourts and later platform mounds, made shell jewellery etched with acid, and traded with Mesoamerica for copper bells and macaws. Large Hohokam settlements were abandoned in the 1400s. The name comes from an O'odham word often translated as 'those who have gone' or 'all used up', and the city of Phoenix was named in 1867 for rising from their ruins.",
 [('Why did they leave?','Archaeologists debate whether floods that destroyed canal intakes, drought, salt build-up in soils, disease or social upheaval ended the Hohokam towns; several factors may have combined.'),
  ('O\'odham memory','O\'odham oral traditions describe a revolt against powerful \'Big House\' chiefs (sivanyi); many O\'odham regard the Hohokam as their ancestors, while archaeologists see continuity as likely but complex.')],
 ['wiki:Hohokam','wiki:Casa_Grande_Ruins_National_Monument'],img='Hohokam',end=1450,dateNote='c. 450–1450 CE (start dates vary)'),
X('marajoara','Marajoara: the mound builders at the mouth of the Amazon',H,'confirmed',3,400,['archaeological'],
 "On Marajó, a huge island at the mouth of the Amazon, people built large earthen mounds (tesos) above the seasonal floods, some of them home to many households, and produced elaborate polychrome pottery, including burial urns painted and modelled with human and animal figures. The culture flourished from roughly the middle of the first millennium CE until about 1400. In the 1940s–50s the archaeologist Betty Meggers argued that Marajoara must have been brought by migrants from the Andes and then declined because the tropical forest could not support complex society. From the 1980s, Anna Roosevelt's excavations showed it developed locally, with dense populations fed by fishing and plant foods.",
 [('Environmental limits','Meggers\' idea that the Amazon was a \'counterfeit paradise\' unable to sustain large societies was influential for decades; it is now widely rejected, though debate continues over how large and hierarchical Marajoara society was.'),
  ('Amazonian chiefdoms','Roosevelt and others interpret Marajoara as a complex chiefdom; some researchers prefer models with less central control, stressing many cooperating mound communities.')],
 ['wiki:Marajoara_culture'],img='Marajoara culture',end=1400,dateNote='roughly 400–1400 CE (estimates vary)'),
X('nuragic-sardinia','The Nuragic civilisation: Sardinia\'s land of 7,000 stone towers',H,'confirmed',2,-1700,['archaeological'],
 "From about 1700 BCE Bronze Age Sardinians built nuraghi, conical dry-stone towers with corbelled vaults, often grouped into fortresses with villages around them; around 7,000 survive, and the complex of Su Nuraxi at Barumini is a UNESCO World Heritage Site. The Nuragic people also built collective burial monuments known as 'giants' graves' (tombe dei giganti), temple wells aligned so that moonlight falls down the stairs at certain times, and made hundreds of small bronze figurines of warriors, chiefs and priestesses. They traded with Mycenaeans, Cypriots and later Phoenicians, and their culture continued until Carthaginian and Roman conquest.",
 [('The Sherden and the Sea Peoples','Some scholars identify the Sherden, a group of Sea Peoples who served and fought Egyptian pharaohs, with Sardinia, based on the similarity of names and horned helmets. Others think the Sherden came from elsewhere and settled in Sardinia later; the link is unproven.'),
  ('Sardinia as Atlantis','The journalist Sergio Frau (2002) argued that the Pillars of Hercules were originally placed at the Strait of Sicily, making Sardinia Plato\'s Atlantis, destroyed by a tsunami. Specialists have not accepted this; it is speculative.')],
 ['wiki:Nuragic_civilization','wiki:Nuraghe','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/833/|UNESCO: Su Nuraxi di Barumini|heritage listing'],img='Nuraghe',dateNote='c. 1700 BCE – Roman conquest (2nd century BCE)'),
X('mont-e-prama','The Giants of Mont\'e Prama: the oldest large statues of the western Mediterranean?',H,'confirmed',2,-900,['archaeological'],
 "In March 1974 a farmer ploughing near Mont'e Prama, on the Sinis peninsula of western Sardinia, struck a stone head. Excavations in 1975–79 recovered about 5,000 sandstone fragments, which have since been restored into statues 2 to 2.6 metres tall of archers, warriors and boxers with large circular eyes, along with models of nuraghe towers; the fragments are now thought to come from 44 statues. They stood near a cemetery of single graves and were later smashed. Their date is debated, from the 11th to the 8th century BCE, but they are among the oldest large free-standing human statues in the western Mediterranean, possibly older than the earliest Greek kouroi. New statues were found in 2014 and 2022.",
 [('Giants of legend','Sardinian folklore speaks of giants who built the nuraghi and the \'giants\' graves\'. The name \'Giants of Mont\'e Prama\' is modern; there is no evidence the statues depict giants, rather than heroised warriors or ancestors.'),
  ('Who smashed them?','Some archaeologists blame Carthaginian conquerors; others suggest internal conflict or ritual destruction. The date and cause of the destruction remain uncertain.')],
 ['wiki:Giants_of_Mont\'e_Prama'],img='Giants of Mont\'e Prama',dateNote='date debated: c. 11th–8th century BCE',changed="1974: discovered by chance; 2014 and 2022: new statues excavated."),
X('talaiotic-menorca','Talaiotic Menorca: taulas, navetas and the Balearic slingers',H,'confirmed',3,-1000,['archaeological'],
 "On Menorca and Mallorca, Iron Age communities from the late second millennium BCE built talaiots (stone towers), large villages, and on Menorca the unique taulas: T-shaped monuments of two huge slabs, standing inside horseshoe-shaped enclosures. The older Naveta des Tudons, a collective tomb shaped like an upturned boat, is one of the best-preserved prehistoric buildings in Europe. In 2023 'Talayotic Menorca' was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Balearic slingers were famous as mercenaries in Carthaginian and Roman armies; the Romans conquered the islands in 123 BCE.",
 [('Star-pointing taulas','The archaeoastronomer Michael Hoskin found that many taula enclosures face south, roughly towards where the Southern Cross and Alpha Centauri rose in antiquity; he suggested a connection with healing cults. Others think orientation reflects local topography.'),
  ('What was a taula?','Interpretations include a symbolic bull\'s head, a stylised god or human figure, a roof support, or an altar or table for offerings; there is no consensus.')],
 ['wiki:Talaiotic_culture','wiki:Taula','wiki:Naveta_d\'Es_Tudons'],img='Taula',end=-123),
X('elam','Elam: Iran\'s first civilisation and the script deciphered in 2022',H,'confirmed',2,-3200,['archaeological','textual'],
 "Elam was a civilisation of south-western Iran, centred on the lowland city of Susa and the highland city of Anshan, from about 3200 BCE until the Persian era. It used two early scripts, Proto-Elamite and Linear Elamite, and later wrote its language, which has no known relatives, in cuneiform. Elam was often at war with Mesopotamia: around 1158 BCE the Elamite king Shutruk-Nakhunte carried off the stele of Hammurabi's laws and the Naram-Sin victory stele to Susa, where French archaeologists found them in 1898–1902. King Untash-Napirisha built the ziggurat of Chogha Zanbil (c. 1250 BCE), and the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal sacked Susa in about 646 BCE. Elamite remained an administrative language of the Persian Empire.",
 [('Linear Elamite decipherment','In 2020–2022 François Desset and colleagues announced a decipherment of Linear Elamite, used around 2300–1880 BCE, based partly on silver vessels with parallel inscriptions. Many specialists accept major parts of it; some question the reliance on vessels of uncertain provenance.'),
  ('Elamo-Dravidian','The linguist David McAlpin proposed that Elamite was related to the Dravidian languages of South India. The idea is not widely accepted but is still discussed.')],
 ['wiki:Elam','wiki:Linear_Elamite','wiki:Chogha_Zanbil'],img='Chogha Zanbil',end=-539),
X('magan','Magan: the copper land of ancient Oman',H,'confirmed',3,-2600,['archaeological','textual'],
 "Mesopotamian texts of the third millennium BCE name three distant lands reached by sea: Dilmun, Magan and Meluhha. Sargon of Akkad boasted that ships from all three moored at his capital, and his grandson Naram-Sin claimed to have defeated a king of Magan. Magan is generally identified with what are now Oman and the United Arab Emirates, which supplied copper mined in the Hajar mountains and diorite for royal statues. The Umm an-Nar culture (c. 2600–2000 BCE) there built round stone towers and large circular communal tombs; the sites of Bat, Al-Khutm and Al-Ayn in Oman are a UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
 [('Where exactly was Magan?','A few scholars suggested Magan included parts of southern Iran or even Egypt in later texts, where the name was reused for Egypt; most now place the third-millennium land in south-eastern Arabia.'),
  ('A link to the Indus','Indus-style seals, weights and pottery in Oman show contact with Meluhha (the Indus civilisation); how much of the trade passed through Magan is debated.')],
 ['wiki:Magan_(civilization)','wiki:Umm_Al_Nar_culture','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/434/|UNESCO: Archaeological Sites of Bat, Al-Khutm and Al-Ayn|heritage listing'],img='Magan (civilization)',dateNote='3rd millennium BCE'),
X('akrotiri','Akrotiri: the Bronze Age town buried by Thera, with no bodies',H,'confirmed',2,-1600,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "Akrotiri was a prosperous Bronze Age town on the island of Santorini (Thera), with multi-storey houses, drains and spectacular wall paintings: a boxing match between boys, a fisherman, swallows among lilies and a long frieze of ships. It was buried in ash and pumice by the great Minoan eruption, now dated by radiocarbon to around 1600 BCE (archaeological chronologies have favoured a slightly later date). Systematic excavation began in 1967 under Spyridon Marinatos, who died at the site in 1974 and is buried there. Unlike Pompeii, almost no human remains and few valuables have been found, suggesting the people left after warning earthquakes.",
 [('Atlantis','Marinatos and the seismologist Angelos Galanopoulos argued that the destruction of Thera and the decline of Minoan Crete inspired Plato\'s Atlantis story. Many find the parallels suggestive, but Plato\'s dates and geography do not fit, and the link is unproven.'),
  ('Where did the people go?','No evacuation site has been identified; it is not known whether the townspeople escaped the island or died in the later phases of the eruption.')],
 ['wiki:Akrotiri_(prehistoric_city)'],img='Akrotiri (prehistoric city)',dateNote='destroyed c. 1600 BCE (date debated)'),
X('ubaid-lizard-figurines','The \'lizard-headed\' figurines of the Ubaid period',H,'disputed',3,-4500,['archaeological','speculative'],
 "The Ubaid period (c. 6500–3800 BCE) in southern Mesopotamia saw the first temples at Eridu and the growth of towns that later became Sumerian cities. From late Ubaid graves and houses at sites such as Ur and Eridu come clay figurines of slender standing figures, male and female, with elongated heads, slanted 'coffee-bean' eyes, and pellets or marks on the shoulders; some women hold or nurse a child. Their meaning is unknown. Archaeologists interpret them as stylised humans, perhaps deities, ancestors or protective figures, and some have noted that skeletons from the period show deliberate head-shaping, which the elongated heads may represent. Their fame has also attracted forgers: Iraq's Sulaymaniyah Museum holds confiscated fake 'lizard-headed' figurines made for the antiquities market.",
 [('Reptilian visitors','Online, the figurines are widely presented as evidence that reptilian beings or ancient astronauts lived among early humans, sometimes linked to the Anunnaki. There is no evidence for this; it reflects modern conspiracy ideas about reptilians, which date mainly from the 1990s.'),
  ('Shoulder marks and tattoos','The pellets on the shoulders have been read as tattoos, scarification, jewellery or symbols of rank; there is no agreement.')],
 ['wiki:Ubaid_period','wiki:Reptilian_conspiracy_theory'],img='File:Lizard-headed nude woman nursing a child, from Ur, Iraq, c. 4000 BCE. Iraq Museum.jpg',dateNote='late Ubaid, c. 4500–4000 BCE'),
]
